      "url": "https://andrewchincea.com/research/water-quality/",
      "title": "🐴 Water-quality prediction on river networks · Andrew Chincea",
      "viewport": [
        1220,
        820
      ],
      "text": "RESEARCH\n\nWater-quality prediction on river networks\nIllustration\nAbout\n\nI use graph-Laplacian regression on river networks to predict hourly water quality at stations that have no data.\n\nI work on RiverNets, a project in the TDA Lab at Kennesaw State University. The model predicts five measurements (water temperature, dissolved oxygen, specific conductance, pH and turbidity) with a smoothness penalty along the river network.\n\nPosition\nUndergraduate Research Assistant – RiverNets (TDA Lab), Kennesaw State University · Aug 2026 – Present\nAdvisor\nDr. Mehmet Aktas\n\nThe Rising water explorer uses elevation data for the same Colorado River Basin study area that RiverNets covers.\n\nMethods\nGraph-Laplacian regression with a smoothness penalty along the river network.\nHourly USGS continuous water-quality records from 28 Colorado River Basin stations, cleaned and audited for completeness by station and variable.\nRiver-network graphs built in NetworkX from MERIT Hydro 90 m flow directions (67,401 nodes for the Colorado basin, compressed to 67), plus graphs for the Chattahoochee basin.\nKron (Schur-complement) reduction that collapses the network exactly onto the station nodes, matching the full Laplacian to within 10⁻⁸ while running about 150× faster.\nBenchmarks against OLS, Ridge, Elastic Net, a spline GAM, random forest and gradient boosting under nested leave-one-station-out validation, plus three-station and whole-subbasin holdouts.\nResults\nAt unseen stations the graph model reached R² = 0.38 for specific conductance and 0.12 for pH, versus 0.02 for the best alternative.\nERA5-Land air temperature raised water-temperature R² from 0.863 to 0.892, and a graph–gradient-boosting blend became the best dissolved-oxygen model (R² = 0.748).\nRelated work\n\nPOSTERAndrew Chincea and Yunus Kaya (Dec 2026). RiverNets: Graph-Laplacian Regression on River Networks for Water Quality Prediction at Unmonitored Stations in the Colorado River Basin. KSU Fall Analytics Day, Kennesaw State University, Kennesaw, GA.\n\nPROJECTAndrew Chincea (Nov 2026). Rising water.\n\nSee this area in the Garden →",
