      "text": "Garden\n\nHere's some of my work. Submitted work has no date yet, so it comes first. The rest is newest first.\n\nTYPE\nAll types\nPapers\nPosters\nTalks\nProjects\nRESEARCH AREA\nAll areas\nWater-quality prediction on river networks\nAnalytic number theory\nSpectral analysis on fractals\n\n9 items\n\nPAPER\nLargest kth-Power Divisor of a Random Integer\nWe establish an asymptotic formula with an O(log x) error term for every integer k ≥ 2.\nSubmitted\nPOSTER\nRiverNets: Graph-Laplacian Regression on River Networks for Water Quality Prediction at Unmonitored Stations in the Colorado River Basin\nWe present graph-Laplacian regression for predicting water quality at unmonitored stations in the Colorado River Basin. · KSU Fall Analytics Day, Kennesaw State University, Kennesaw, GA\nDec 2026\nPROJECT\nOwlPath\nAn interactive prerequisite map for three KSU majors. Students mark the courses they've finished and see which classes they can take next term.\nNov 2026 – Present\nPROJECT\nPersonal portfolio\nMy academic site for research, projects and CV.\nNov 2026\nPROJECT\nRising water\nAn interactive explorer of persistent homology: water rises over Colorado River Basin terrain while a barcode updates live.\nNov 2026\nTALK\nLargest Square Divisor of a Random Integer\nWe presented the square-divisor case (k = 2) of the largest kth-power divisor of a random integer. · Statistics & Data Applications session, 105th MAA Southeastern Section Meeting, University of North Alabama, Florence, AL\nMar 2026\nPROJECT\nNYC Subway Incident Modeling\nRegression models in R and Quarto that relate weather and ridership to NYC subway disruptions, using MTA major-incident records for 2015–2024.\nJan 2026 – May 2026\nPOSTER\nProperties of Eigenvalues of Fractal Laplacian\nResults from my First-Year Scholar research on eigenvalues of the fractal Laplacian. · 104th MAA Southeastern Section Meeting, High Point University, High Point, NC\nMar 2025\nPROJECT\nAskScrappy\nAn academic-advising assistant built with LangChain, Ollama and LLaMA 3.1. It uses retrieval-augmented generation and runs entirely on local hardware.\nAug 2024 – Dec 2024",
